{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>All who were counted of the Levites, etc.</b> Ramban, of blessed memory, concluded with a matter about which one may wonder: for the Levites are the beloved ones of HaShem and His intimates, who did not sin with the calf and were not stricken and were not killed and were not subjected [to forced labor] — and how were they diminished so greatly compared to all the tribes, such that their number from a month old does not reach [even] half the smallest tribe in Israel? And when you examine their number from twenty years old, they do not reach a third or a quarter. And I saw that one may settle [the question] in two ways: the first is on account of the multiplicity that came from the affliction, as it is written (Exodus 1:12): ״וְכַאֲשֶׁר יְעַנּוּ אוֹתוֹ כֵּן יִרְבֶּה״ etc. And a second way is because of the anger of Jacob our father, peace be upon him. And in my eyes the second reason is far-fetched, for no trace of a diminution of seed is found [as a result of] the anger of their father, peace be upon him. Further, you will find in the number given in Chronicles (I Chronicles 23:3) that the Levites were thirty-eight thousand from thirty years old to fifty years old, while their number in the portion of Naso came to a total of eight thousand five hundred and eighty — so they had increased approximately fourfold over this number, while in the count of the children of Israel given there, they did not reach double the number stated here [in our passage]. And estimating this number, were they to be counted from twenty to a hundred and above in the manner that Israel is counted, their number could equal [that of] the larger tribes of Israel and beyond — and where then is their father's anger?" "<b>And</b> the first reason, of the affliction, is a sound reason, but it would not be justified according to the statement of Hazal (Genesis Rabbah 79:1) who said: Jacob did not die until Israel reached the full number of sixty myriads. And one must necessarily reconcile the verses according to their words, may their memory be blessed, for [the verse] says ״כֵּן יִרְבֶּה״ — meaning, just as in the initial rate, so would they multiply, and they would not diminish even though they would kill from among them and afflict them; there was power in Israel to be fruitful and multiply according to the initial rate to fill what was lacking. And what the verse says (Exodus 1:7) ״וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל פָּרוּ״ etc. ״וַיָּקֻצוּ מִפְּנֵי״ etc., is because in the days of Jacob they were very young and made no impression in the city, and when they grew up they became widely known. From this point on, the multiplication preceded the affliction — so why should it not have been likewise for the tribe of Levi? And one cannot say that they were likewise as numerous as the other tribes at the time of their father's passing and diminished afterward, and because the affliction was not upon them they did not multiply again — for this too I ask: why should they have diminished from the original blessing? And Hazal said (Numbers Rabbah 5:1) that the Ark consumed among them; that was after the appointment of the Levites' service." "<b>And what is correct</b> in my eyes is this: the tribe of Levi had one practice, like the practice of Amram, who sent away his wife because of the decree against the newborn son. And the reason is that since they were pampered and were not enslaved, their hearts did not melt so completely [as] to see their plantings cast into the river — as did an Israelite man who, on account of the servitude and the yoke of labor, was cheapened and made light of in their eyes and in their hearts to see their children cast into the river. And they even treated themselves contemptibly by giving birth and abandoning [the children] in the fields and ditches of the land — which a respected person, whose soul is precious to him, would not do, whereas the tribe of Levi did so. And this is your sign: [the fact] that Amram sent away his wife — and the verse had no need to inform us of the matter, for even regarding Amram the verse did not state the matter; rather, it became known from the reasoning of the situation, and it teaches about the entire tribe. And this is the cause of their paucity, for if there are no kids there are no goats. And even though we find that Amram took back his divorcee, one should not infer from him that all of them took [their wives] back, for he alone is the one to whom the matter came through prophecy, as Hazal said in the Aggadah (Exodus Rabbah ch. 1, Sotah 12a) and in the holy Zohar (vol. II 11a) on the verse ״וַיֵּלֶךְ אִישׁ״ etc., and as they also said (Exodus Rabbah ch. 1) on the verse ״וַתֵּתַצַּב אֲחֹתוֹ״ etc. — that she went to see what would become of her prophecy." "And if you should say: why would a tribe of Torah scholars diminish [in the matter of] procreation — perhaps they reasoned thus in order not ״to labor in vain״ and not ״to give birth in terror״ (Isaiah 65:23), and similar to this we find that they said (Ta'anit 11a) that it is forbidden to engage one's bed in a year of famine even though there is a prevention of procreation, and they reasoned likewise: because of the annihilation of the male children they refrained entirely. And from this point on, on the contrary, HaShem performed a miracle for them in that this number remained from them; and therefore, after a time when they too engaged in procreation, they ״were fruitful and swarmed״ etc. more than the rest of the tribes."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:15.515667090Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}